Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>12  Appointments and anniversaries</H1>
<HR>
The diary can show the time four ways, but so far there is nothing in it you didn't put there with a text
editor. This chapter makes it a real diary. You'll add appointments with a dialogue box, change them, make them
repeat every week or every month, choose them, drag them to another day and throw them into the wastebasket.
The Anniversary section gets its pages too, and each birthday also appears in the diary on its day, so it
needn't be written twice. The program is <CODE>!Org12</CODE>.<P>
It's the biggest step of Part Two, because choosing, deleting and dragging are written once, for every
section: the To do list, the notepad and the planner will get them for nothing in the chapters that follow.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Appointments</H2>
An appointment is a plain object, like a contact. The comment at the start of the diary's part of
<CODE>Store</CODE> says what's in one:<P>
<PRE>
// ------------------------------------------------ the diary
// An appointment: { date, time ('09:30' or ''), end, text, alarm
// (minutes before, or null), repeat: { every: 'day'|'week'|'month'|
// 'year', count, until } or null }.
const STEP = { day: D.addDays, week: (k, n) =&gt; D.addDays(k, 7 * n),
  month: D.addMonths, year: D.addYears };
</PRE>
<CODE>date</CODE> is a key, as in chapter 11, and <CODE>time</CODE> and <CODE>end</CODE> are kept the same
way: <CODE>'09:30'</CODE>, always with two digits for the hour, so that times sort as text too. An
appointment with no time (a day out, say) has an empty one. <CODE>alarm</CODE> is how many minutes before the
appointment to be reminded; nothing looks at it yet (that's chapter 16). <CODE>repeat</CODE> is
<CODE>null</CODE> for an appointment that happens once. <CODE>STEP</CODE> comes into it later in the
chapter.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>The Appointment box</H2>
Appointments are made and changed in a dialogue box built with chapter 3's <CODE>Form</CODE>. The boxes for
all the organiser's entries live in one module, <CODE>Entries</CODE>, which knows nothing about windows or
binders: each function takes an entry, shows the box, and resolves to the new fields, or <CODE>null</CODE> if
you chose Cancel.<P>
<PRE>
// Entries - the dialogue boxes for the organiser's entries, made with
// the toolkit's Form. Each resolves to the entry's new fields, or
// null if Cancel was chosen.
//
//   await editAppointment(task, appt)   { date, time, end, text,
//                                          alarm, repeat }
//   await editAnniversary(task, a)      { date, text }
import { Form } from './Toolkit/Form';
import * as D from './Toolkit/Dates';

// dates are shown as 25/09/2026, and kept as 2026-09-25
export const showDay = (key) =&gt; (key ? D.format(key, '%DY/%MN/%CE%YR')
  : '');
const forms = new Map();                 // each box is made once
function form(task, name, spec) {
  if (!forms.has(name)) forms.set(name, new Form(task, spec));
  return forms.get(name);
}

// ------------------------------------------------ appointments
const ALARMS = [['None', null], ['At the time', 0], ['5 minutes', 5],
  ['15 minutes', 15], ['1 hour', 60], ['1 day', 1440]];
const REPEATS = [['Never', null], ['Every day', 'day'],
  ['Every week', 'week'], ['Every month', 'month'],
  ['Every year', 'year']];
const nameOf = (list, v) =&gt; list.find(([, x]) =&gt; x === v)?.[0];
const valueOf = (list, s) =&gt; list.find(([n]) =&gt; n === s)?.[1] ?? null;

</PRE>
The diary keeps <CODE>'2026-09-25'</CODE>, but people type 25/09/2026, so <CODE>showDay</CODE> turns a key
into what the box shows, and <CODE>D.parseDay</CODE> (chapter 11) turns what was typed back into a key.<P>
Chapter 3's address book made its one box when it was first needed, with <CODE>form ??=
makeForm(task)</CODE>. The organiser will have several boxes, so <CODE>form</CODE> keeps them in a
<CODE>Map</CODE> by name and makes each the first time it is asked for. After that the same box is used again,
already built.<P>
The Alarm and Repeat fields are pop-up menus, and each choice on them stands for a value in the entry:
"15 minutes" is 15, "Every week" is <CODE>'week'</CODE>. <CODE>ALARMS</CODE> and <CODE>REPEATS</CODE> are
lists of <CODE>[name, value]</CODE> pairs, and two little functions look things up in them either way round.
<CODE>valueOf</CODE> finds the pair whose first item is the name chosen, and gives its second. In
<CODE>nameOf</CODE>, the function's argument is <CODE>[, x]</CODE>: a pair, with its first item skipped (the
comma with nothing before it, as in chapter 8's <CODE>[last, first, , title]</CODE>), and its second called
<CODE>x</CODE>. <CODE>?.[0]</CODE> then takes the name from the pair found - or gives <CODE>undefined</CODE>,
rather than an error, if there wasn't one. (<CODE>?.</CODE> works before square brackets just as it does
before a property name.)<P>
<PRE>
export async function editAppointment(task, a) {
  const f = form(task, 'appt', {
    title: 'Appointment', buttons: ['Cancel', 'OK'],
    help: 'Type the appointment\'s details and click OK.',
    fields: [
      { name: 'date', label: 'Date', width: 120,
        help: 'The day, as 25/09/2026.' },
      { name: 'time', label: 'Time', width: 70,
        help: 'When it starts, as 9:30 (or leave it empty).' },
      { name: 'end', label: 'Until', width: 70 },
      { name: 'text', label: 'What', lines: 2 },
      { name: 'alarm', label: 'Alarm', width: 130,
        choices: ALARMS.map(([n]) =&gt; n),
        help: 'How long before it the alarm goes off.' },
      { name: 'repeat', label: 'Repeat', width: 130,
        choices: REPEATS.map(([n]) =&gt; n) },
      { name: 'count', label: 'Times', width: 60, allowed: '0-9',
        help: 'How many times it happens (empty: for ever).' },
    ],
    check: (v) =&gt; {
      if (!D.parseDay(v.date)) return { field: 'date',
        message: 'Give the date as 25/09/2026' };
      for (const f of ['time', 'end']) {
        if (v[f] &amp;&amp; !D.parseTime(v[f])) return { field: f,
          message: 'Give the time as 9:30' };
      }
      if (!v.text.trim()) return { field: 'text',
        message: 'What is the appointment?' };
      return null;
    },
  });
  const v = await f.ask({ ...a, date: showDay(a.date),
    alarm: nameOf(ALARMS, a.alarm ?? null),
    repeat: nameOf(REPEATS, a.repeat?.every ?? null),
    count: a.repeat?.count ?? '' });
  if (!v) return null;
  const every = valueOf(REPEATS, v.repeat);
  return {
    date: D.parseDay(v.date), text: v.text.trim(),
    time: D.parseTime(v.time) ?? '', end: D.parseTime(v.end) ?? '',
    alarm: valueOf(ALARMS, v.alarm),
    repeat: every ? { every, count: +v.count || null } : null,
  };
}
</PRE>
The fields are chapter 3's; <CODE>width</CODE> makes the date and time fields narrower than usual. <CODE>check</CODE> is called when you click OK; returning an object keeps the box open,
with the message given and the caret in the field that's wrong. It uses <CODE>parseDay</CODE> and
<CODE>parseTime</CODE> only to see whether they work: <CODE>31/02/2026</CODE> is refused, and so is
<CODE>25:00</CODE>.<P>
<CODE>ask</CODE> is given a copy of the appointment with each value turned into what the box shows: the
date as 23/09/2026, the alarm and repeat as the names on their menus, and the count of repeats (or nothing).
When the box closes, the same happens the other way. <CODE>+v.count || null</CODE> turns the Times field into a
number, and an empty field (which <CODE>+</CODE> makes 0) into <CODE>null</CODE>, meaning "for ever".<P>
