<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Adding and changing</H2>
The diary page decides what a double-click means:<P>
<PRE>
  async double(ev) {
    if (this.view === 'Year') return;        // (its week opens)
    this.opening?.();                        // not a click: stay here
    const box = this.at(ev.x, ev.y);
    if (box?.ref.section === 'diary') return this.edit(box.ref);
    if (box) return this.org.go(box.ref);        // someone else's
    const day = this.dayAt(ev.x, ev.y);
    if (day) await this.add(day);
  }
</PRE>
On an appointment, it changes that appointment. On something that belongs to another section - an
anniversary, later in this chapter - it goes to that section, with the window's <CODE>go</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
  /** Show an entry, in whichever section it is. */
  go(ref) {
    const i = this.sections.findIndex((s) =&gt; s.id === ref.section);
    this.open(i);
    this.section.go(ref);
    this.selected = ref;
    this.refresh();
  }
</PRE>
which opens the entry's section, asks the section to show the entry (each section's own <CODE>go</CODE>)
and chooses it. On an empty part of a day it adds an appointment on that day. (Not in the Year view, whose days are too
small to aim at; clicking one there goes to its week, as in chapter 11.)<P>
There's a catch in the Month view. In chapter 11 a click on a day there went straight to its week - but a
double-click starts with a click, so by the time the double-click arrived the page would already be showing
the week, and the appointment would be added on the wrong day. So a click in the Month or Year view now waits
before going to the week:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ the mouse
  click(ev) {
    const day = this.dayAt(ev.x, ev.y);
    if (!day) return false;
    this.day = day;
    if (this.view === 'Month' || this.view === 'Year') {
      // a date: see its week - in a moment, as this may be the first
      // click of a double-click (to add an appointment on this page)
      this.opening?.();
      this.opening = this.org.app.task.after(
        os.input.config.doubleClickMs, () =&gt; this.openWeek());
    }
    return true;
  }
  openWeek() {
    this.setView('Week');
    this.org.refresh();
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>task.after</CODE> (the first book's chapter 9) calls <CODE>openWeek</CODE> once the double-click time
has passed - <CODE>os.input.config.doubleClickMs</CODE>, the time the desktop allows between the two clicks of
a double-click - and returns a function that cancels it. <CODE>double</CODE> calls that function,
<CODE>this.opening?.()</CODE>, before anything else, so a double-click stays on the page it was made on. (In the
Year view <CODE>double</CODE> returns straight away, and the week still opens.)<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ entries
  async add(day = this.day) {
    const v = await editAppointment(this.org.app.task, { date: day });
    this.org.focus();
    if (v) this.doc.diary.add(v);
  }
  async edit(ref) {
    const a = this.doc.diary.get(ref.id);
    const v = await editAppointment(this.org.app.task, a);
    this.org.focus();
    if (v) this.doc.diary.update(a.id, v);
  }
  newEntry() { this.add(this.day); }
  describe(ref) {
    const a = this.doc.diary.get(ref.id);
    return a ? `${showDay(ref.date ?? a.date)} ${a.time} ${a.text}`
      .replace(/ +/g, ' ') : 'Appointment';
  }
  remove(ref) { this.doc.diary.remove(ref.id); }
</PRE>
<CODE>add</CODE> and <CODE>edit</CODE> are nearly the same: show the box with an appointment - a new one with
just a date, or the one being changed - and if you clicked OK, add or update it. There's no need to redraw
anything: the diary is a <CODE>Collection</CODE>, which emits <CODE>'change'</CODE>, and the window listens
for that and redraws (chapter 10). After the box closes, <CODE>this.org.focus()</CODE> gives the input focus
back to the binder, so the keys work straight away.<P>
<CODE>newEntry</CODE> is what the window calls for the Insert key, the New entry menu item and the new button at
the top of the toolbox: add an appointment on the day you're looking at. The Address section already had a
<CODE>newEntry</CODE>, which adds a person (chapter 10), so the same key, menu item and button work there
too. <CODE>describe</CODE> and <CODE>remove</CODE> are for deleting, which comes shortly; the Address
section gets the same two methods in this chapter, so from now on contacts can be deleted too:<P>
<PRE>
  describe(ref) {
    const c = this.doc.book.get(ref.id);
    return c ? nameOf(c) : 'Contact';
  }
  remove(ref) {
    this.doc.book.remove(ref.id);
    if (this.chosen === ref.id) this.chosen = null;
  }
</PRE>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Repeats</H2>
A weekly meeting could be written into the diary 52 times, but then moving it would mean changing 52
appointments. Instead, a repeating appointment is kept once, with a <CODE>repeat</CODE> saying how often,
and the diary works out which days it falls on whenever it's drawn:<P>
<PRE>
export class Diary extends Collection {
  /** [{ appt, date }] for the days from..to, in time order. */
  occurrences(from, to) {
    const out = [];
    for (const a of this.all()) {
      for (const date of datesOf(a, from, to)) {
        out.push({ appt: a, date });
      }
    }
    return out.sort((x, y) =&gt; (x.date + (x.appt.time || ''))
      .localeCompare(y.date + (y.appt.time || '')));
  }
}
/** The days an appointment falls on between from and to. */
function datesOf(a, from, to) {
  if (!a.repeat) {
    return a.date &gt;= from &amp;&amp; a.date &lt;= to ? [a.date] : [];
  }
  const step = STEP[a.repeat.every] ?? STEP.week;
  const out = [];
  for (let n = 0, d = a.date; d &lt;= to; d = step(a.date, ++n)) {
    if (a.repeat.count &amp;&amp; n &gt;= a.repeat.count) break;
    if (a.repeat.until &amp;&amp; d &gt; a.repeat.until) break;
    if (d &gt;= from) out.push(d);
    if (n &gt; 5000) break;               // (a guard, just in case)
  }
  return out;
}
</PRE>
<CODE>occurrences(from, to)</CODE> is what the views ask for: every appointment on the days from
<CODE>from</CODE> to <CODE>to</CODE>, each with the day it falls on (so one weekly meeting can appear four
times in a month), sorted by date and then time. Because keys and times both sort as text, gluing them
together (<CODE>'2026-09-21' + '09:30'</CODE>) gives something that sorts by both at once.<P>
<CODE>datesOf</CODE> does the work for one appointment. One that doesn't repeat is on one day or none. For a
repeating one, <CODE>STEP</CODE> gives the function that goes on by <CODE>n</CODE> steps:
<CODE>D.addDays</CODE> for daily, <CODE>D.addMonths</CODE> for monthly and so on - chapter 11's
functions, with weekly made from <CODE>addDays</CODE>. The loop counts <CODE>n</CODE> up from 0, and stops
when the date passes <CODE>to</CODE>, when it has happened <CODE>count</CODE> times or when it passes the date
<CODE>until</CODE>; dates before <CODE>from</CODE> are counted but not kept. The last line is a guard: a
daily appointment started years ago would still finish, but if a mistake ever made the dates stop moving,
the loop couldn't run for ever.<P>
Notice that each date is worked out from the <I>first</I> date, <CODE>step(a.date, ++n)</CODE>, not from the
one before. It matters for months. Something on the 31st of each month falls on 28 February, because there
is no 31st; if March's date were worked out from February's, it would be the 28th too, and so would every
month after. Worked out from the start, March is back on the 31st. That's the kind of thing to write down as a
test before trusting it, and <CODE>Check</CODE> has grown some:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ the diary
  const diary = new Diary();
  diary.add({ date: '2026-09-21', time: '09:30', text: 'Meeting',
    repeat: { every: 'week', count: 3 } });
  diary.add({ date: '2026-09-23', time: '08:00', text: 'Dentist' });
  check('repeats, within a count',
    diary.occurrences('2026-09-01', '2026-12-31')
      .map((o) =&gt; o.date).filter((d) =&gt; d !== '2026-09-23'),
    ['2026-09-21', '2026-09-28', '2026-10-05']);
  check('in date and time order', diary.occurrences('2026-09-21',
    '2026-09-27').map((o) =&gt; o.appt.text), ['Meeting', 'Dentist']);
  const monthly = new Diary([{ id: 1, date: '2026-01-31', text: 'Pay',
    repeat: { every: 'month' } }]);
  check('monthly on the 31st', monthly.occurrences('2026-02-01',
    '2026-04-30').map((o) =&gt; o.date),
  ['2026-02-28', '2026-03-31', '2026-04-30']);
</PRE>
