<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Choosing and deleting</H2>
To delete an entry you first say which: click it, and it's outlined. Choosing works the same in every
section, so it's in <CODE>Tools</CODE>, not in the diary:<P>
<PRE>
// ------------------------------------------------ choosing entries
/** A click on an entry: choose it. */
export function clickEntry(org, ev) {
  const box = org.section.at(ev.x, ev.y);
  if (!box) {
    org.selected = null;
    return false;
  }
  org.selected = box.ref;
  org.redraw();
  return false;                           // the section sees it too
}
/** After the section has drawn: the chosen entry. */
export function decorate(org, g) {
  const same = (a, b) =&gt; a &amp;&amp; b &amp;&amp; a.section === b.section &amp;&amp;
    a.id === b.id &amp;&amp; (a.date ?? '') === (b.date ?? '');
  for (const b of org.section.boxes) {
    if (same(b.ref, org.selected)) {
      g.strokeStyle = '#000000';
      g.lineWidth = 2;
      g.strokeRect(b.x - 1, b.y - 1, b.w + 2, b.h + 2);
    }
  }
}
</PRE>
<CODE>org.selected</CODE> is the chosen entry's ref: <CODE>{ section: 'diary', id: 4, date: '2026-09-21' }</CODE>
say. Every section records a box for each entry it draws (chapter 10), so <CODE>clickEntry</CODE> only has to
ask the section what's at the point clicked. It returns <CODE>false</CODE>, so the window goes on to give the
click to the section as well (the diary also makes that day the chosen day):<P>
<PRE>
    if (tools.clickEntry(this, ev)) return true;
    this.section.click(ev);
</PRE>
When the window has drawn a section, it calls <CODE>decorate</CODE> to outline the chosen entry. A repeating
appointment has one box for each day it's shown on, but the ref of each box includes its date, so only the
one you clicked is outlined: two refs are the <CODE>same</CODE> if they have the same section, id and date (an
entry with no date gives <CODE>''</CODE> for both).<P>
Deleting is the Delete key (or Backspace), the Delete menu item or a click on the wastebasket:<P>
<PRE>
      case 0x1CD: this.section.newEntry(); return true;      // Insert
      case 127: case 8: tools.remove(this); return true;     // Delete
</PRE>
<PRE>
// ------------------------------------------------ deleting
export async function remove(org, ref = org.selected) {
  if (!ref) { beep(); return; }
  const s = org.sections.find((x) =&gt; x.id === ref.section);
  if (org.app.prefs.confirm) {
    const r = await query({ task: org.app.task, title: 'Organiser',
      message: `Delete '${s.describe(ref)}'?`,
      buttons: ['Delete', 'Cancel'] });
    org.focus();
    if (r !== 'Delete') return;
  }
  s.remove(ref);
  org.selected = null;
  org.refresh();
}
</PRE>
If nothing is chosen, <CODE>beep</CODE> says so. Otherwise <CODE>remove</CODE> finds the section the entry
belongs to - which needn't be the one on show: the diary shows anniversaries too, as you'll see - and,
unless <CODE>prefs.confirm</CODE> says not to (chapter 16's choices can turn it off), asks first. <CODE>query</CODE> is the desktop's question box:
it shows the message and buttons and resolves to the name of the button clicked. The question needs a line
about the entry, and only its section knows how to write one; that's <CODE>describe</CODE>. Then the
section's <CODE>remove</CODE> deletes it.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/O12Delete" WIDTH=910 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>
Deleting a repeating appointment deletes all of it, every week: the question shows the date you clicked, but
there's only one entry to delete. (A diary program with more time could ask "just this one, or all of them?"
and keep a list of the dates left out.)<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Dragging</H2>
The window's work area has been reporting drags since chapter 10 (<CODE>workButton:
'clickdragdouble'</CODE>), and now something listens:<P>
<PRE>
    w.on('drag', (ev) =&gt; tools.drag(this, ev));
</PRE>
<PRE>
// ------------------------------------------------ dragging entries
/** A drag on an entry: into the wastebasket, or somewhere else. */
export async function drag(org, ev) {
  const box = org.section.at(ev.x, ev.y);
  if (!box) { org.section.drag(ev); return true; }
  const p = org.win.workToScreen(box.x, box.y);
  const drop = await wimp.drag({ event: ev.pointerEvent,
    box: { x0: p.x, y0: p.y, x1: p.x + box.w, y1: p.y + box.h } });
  if (drop.window === org.bar.pane &amp;&amp; drop.icon?.name === 'bin') {
    await remove(org, box.ref);
  } else if (drop.window === org.win) {
    org.section.dropped(box.ref, drop.x, drop.y);
    org.refresh();
  }
  return true;
}
</PRE>
If the drag didn't start on an entry, the section is given it, in case it wants to do something else (none
does yet). Otherwise <CODE>wimp.drag</CODE> drags a dotted box the size of the entry, following the pointer,
the way the Filer drags a file's box. It needs the box in <I>screen</I> coordinates, so the entry's
position in the work area is turned into one with <CODE>workToScreen</CODE>. It's an <CODE>async</CODE>
function: it resolves when the button is let go, with where that was - the <CODE>window</CODE> and
<CODE>icon</CODE> under the pointer, and <CODE>x</CODE> and <CODE>y</CODE> in that window's work area -
plus whether Shift or Ctrl was held down.<P>
Dropped on the wastebasket - the icon called <CODE>bin</CODE> in the toolbox, which is a pane of its own -
the entry is deleted, just as if it had been chosen and the wastebasket clicked. Dropped anywhere on the
binder, the section is asked to put the entry there:<P>
<PRE>
  /** An entry dropped on a day: move it there. */
  dropped(ref, x, y) {
    const day = this.dayAt(x, y);
    if (!day) return;
    const doc = this.doc;
    if (ref.section === 'diary') {
      const a = doc.diary.get(ref.id);
      // a repeating appointment moves as a whole: by the same days
      const shift = D.daysBetween(ref.date ?? a.date, day);
      doc.diary.update(a.id, { date: D.addDays(a.date, shift) });
    }
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>dayAt</CODE> (chapter 11) says which day is under the point. A repeating appointment moves as a whole,
by the same number of days as the one you dragged: drag Monday's meeting to Tuesday and it's on Tuesdays from
then on, from its first week. The <CODE>shift</CODE> is worked out from <CODE>ref.date</CODE>, the day you
dragged it from, and then applied to <CODE>a.date</CODE>, the day it started.<P>
Dropping anything else - an anniversary, from its flag in the diary - does nothing: an anniversary's day is its
date, and it belongs to its own section.<P>
<H3>Pictures in the toolbox</H3>
The new button and the wastebasket are pictures, not words, done exactly as !Contacts' toolbar did it in
chapter 5: the pictures are in the program's own <CODE>Sprites</CODE> file, which <CODE>!RunImage</CODE>
loads into a sprite area when it starts,<P>
<PRE>
  const app = {
    task, TYPE,
    docs: new Set(),                 // the open organiser files
    prefs: { colour: '#6b3f22', confirm: true },  // binder; ask
    area: sprites.spritesFromFile(await vfs.readFile(ctx.dir +
      '.Sprites')),                  // the toolbox's pictures
</PRE>
and a button with <CODE>sprite: 'tb_bin'</CODE> shows that sprite from it:<P>
<PRE>
// ------------------------------------------------ the toolbox
const VIEWS = 4;                          // room for 4 view buttons
export function buttons(org) {
  const view = (i) =&gt; ({ name: 'view' + i, text: '', h: 26,
    action: () =&gt; { const s = org.section;
      s.setView(s.views[i]); org.refresh(); } });
  return [
    { name: 'new', sprite: 'tb_new', action: () =&gt; org.section
      .newEntry(), help: 'Click SELECT to add an entry here.' },
    { name: 'today', text: TODAY(), h: 30,
      action: () =&gt; { org.section.today(); org.refresh(); },
      help: 'Today\'s date. Click SELECT to go to today.' },
    { gap: true },
    ...Array.from({ length: VIEWS }, (_, i) =&gt; view(i)),
    { gap: true },
    { name: 'bin', sprite: 'tb_bin', action: () =&gt; remove(org),
      help: 'The wastebasket: drag an entry here to delete it, ' +
        'or click SELECT to delete the chosen entry.' },
  ];
}
</PRE>
<CODE>updateToolbox</CODE> now also changes the new button's help to suit the section: "add an
appointment" in the Diary, "add a person to the address book" in Address.<P>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Anniversaries</H2>
Anniversaries are the simplest entries: a date and some text. The date is the one it all began - a
birthday's is the day someone was born - so the organiser can work out how many years it has been.<P>
<PRE>
// ------------------------------------------------ the other sections
// An anniversary: { date ('1968-03-14': the year it began), text }
export class Anniversaries extends Collection {
  /** Those in a month (1-12), in day order. */
  inMonth(month) {
    return this.all().filter((a) =&gt; +a.date.slice(5, 7) === month)
      .sort((a, b) =&gt; a.date.slice(8).localeCompare(b.date.slice(8)));
  }
  onDay(date) {
    const md = date.slice(5);                   // the month and day
    return this.all().filter((a) =&gt; a.date.slice(5) === md);
  }
}
/** '58th' - how many years it has been, in a given year. */
export function nth(a, year) {
  const n = year - +a.date.slice(0, 4);
  if (n &lt;= 0) return '';
  const t = n % 100;
  const s = t &gt;= 11 &amp;&amp; t &lt;= 13 ? 'th'
    : ['th', 'st', 'nd', 'rd'][n % 10] ?? 'th';
  return n + s;
}
</PRE>
<CODE>inMonth</CODE> and <CODE>onDay</CODE> ignore the year: <CODE>a.date.slice(5, 7)</CODE> is the month
(<CODE>'03'</CODE>, which <CODE>+</CODE> turns into 3), and <CODE>slice(5)</CODE> is the month and day,
<CODE>'03-14'</CODE>, the same every year.<P>
<CODE>nth</CODE> gives the English for how many years: 1st, 2nd, 3rd, then 4th to 20th, then 21st, 22nd,
23rd, and so on. <CODE>['th', 'st', 'nd', 'rd'][n % 10]</CODE> looks up the last digit; for 4 to 9 there is
nothing in the list, and <CODE>??</CODE> gives <CODE>'th'</CODE>. 11, 12 and 13 are the exceptions (11th,
not 11st), and so are 111, 112 and so on, which is why the test is on <CODE>n % 100</CODE>.<P>
The Anniversary section is a real section now, in place of chapter 10's blank one. Its pages have a box for
each month, January to June on the left, July to December on the right:<P>
<PRE>
  draw(g, L, R) {
    heading(g, L, 'Anniversary', this.colour, String(this.year));
    heading(g, R, 'Anniversary', this.colour, String(this.year));
    this.dayLinks = [];
    [[L, 1], [R, 7]].forEach(([p, m0]) =&gt; {
      const bw = (p.w - 30) / 2, bh = (p.h - 56) / 3;
      for (let i = 0; i &lt; 6; i++) {
        const x = p.x + 10 + (i % 2) * (bw + 10);
        const y = p.y + 46 + Math.floor(i / 2) * bh;
        this.month(g, m0 + i, x, y, bw, bh - 8);
      }
    });
  }
  month(g, m, x, y, w, h) {
    g.strokeStyle = RULE;
    g.strokeRect(x + 0.5, y + 0.5, w - 1, h - 1);
    g.fillStyle = this.colour;
    g.fillRect(x, y, w, 18);
    const first = `${this.year}-${String(m).padStart(2, '0')}-01`;
    text(g, D.format(first, '%MO'), x + 6, y + 9, w - 12,
      'bold 12px Homerton', '#ffffff');
    let ly = y + 22;
    for (const a of this.doc.anniversaries.inMonth(m)) {
      if (ly + LINE &gt; y + h) {                   // no more room
        text(g, '...', x + w - 18, ly, 14);
        break;
      }
      const day = +a.date.slice(8), n = nth(a, this.year);
      text(g, String(day), x + 6, ly + LINE / 2, 20,
        'bold 12px Homerton', '#1c4f8a');
      this.dayLinks.push({ x: x + 4, y: ly, w: 20, h: LINE,
        date: `${this.year}-${a.date.slice(5)}` });
      text(g, a.text + (n ? ` (${n})` : ''), x + 28, ly + LINE / 2,
        w - 34, '12px Homerton', INK);
      this.box(x + 26, ly, w - 30, LINE, { id: a.id });
      ly += LINE;
    }
    if (ly === y + 22) {
      text(g, '-', x + 8, ly + LINE / 2, 20, '12px Homerton', FAINT);
    }
  }
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/O12Anniversary" WIDTH=910 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>
Each anniversary records its box as it's drawn, so that it can be chosen, deleted and dragged into the
wastebasket like any entry. The day numbers are recorded too, in <CODE>dayLinks</CODE>: click one and the
diary opens at that day. Page Up and Page Down go a year back and on, and change the (58th)s. The rest of the
class is short, because it's the diary's methods over again:<P>
<PRE>
  click(ev) {
    const d = this.dayLinks.find((b) =&gt; inside(b, ev.x, ev.y));
    if (d) { this.org.go({ section: 'diary', date: d.date }); }
    return true;
  }
  async double(ev) {
    const box = this.at(ev.x, ev.y);
    if (!box) return;
    const a = this.doc.anniversaries.get(box.ref.id);
    const v = await editAnniversary(this.org.app.task, a);
    this.org.focus();
    if (v) this.doc.anniversaries.update(a.id, v);
  }
  async newEntry() {
    const v = await editAnniversary(this.org.app.task, {});
    this.org.focus();
    if (v) this.doc.anniversaries.add(v);
  }
  turn(dir) { this.year += dir; }
  today() { this.year = +D.today().slice(0, 4); }
  describe(ref) {
    const a = this.doc.anniversaries.get(ref.id);
    return a ? `${showDay(a.date)} ${a.text}` : 'Anniversary';
  }
  remove(ref) { this.doc.anniversaries.remove(ref.id); }
  go() { this.year = +D.today().slice(0, 4); }
</PRE>
<H3>Anniversaries in the diary</H3>
Birthdays are no use in a section you don't look at, so the diary shows them too. Each view draws a day's
entries with the diary's <CODE>itemsOn</CODE>, which now gives the day's anniversaries first, then its
appointments:<P>
<PRE>
  /** Everything to show on a day: anniversaries, then
   * appointments, as { kind, text, ref, time? }. */
  itemsOn(key) {
    const doc = this.doc, year = +key.slice(0, 4);
    return [
      ...doc.anniversaries.onDay(key).map((a) =&gt; ({
        kind: 'anniversary',
        text: a.text + (nth(a, year) ? ` (${nth(a, year)})` : ''),
        ref: { section: 'anniversary', id: a.id } })),
      ...doc.diary.occurrences(key, key).map(({ appt }) =&gt; ({
        kind: 'appt', time: appt.time, text: appt.text,
        ref: { id: appt.id, date: key } })),
    ];
  }
</PRE>
Each item carries a ref for its box. An appointment's is <CODE>{ id, date }</CODE>, and <CODE>box</CODE>
adds <CODE>section: 'diary'</CODE>. An anniversary's says <CODE>section: 'anniversary'</CODE> itself; since
<CODE>box</CODE> writes <CODE>{ section: this.id, ...ref }</CODE>, the ref's own section comes second and
wins. So the diary can show another section's entry, and everything else does the right thing with it: a
double-click goes to the Anniversary section (the <CODE>org.go</CODE> in <CODE>double</CODE>), and Delete
asks the Anniversary section to describe and remove it. The views draw an anniversary with a little flag in
its colour instead of a time:<P>
<PRE>
/** One entry: an anniversary, or a time. */
function item(diary, g, it, x, y, w) {
  const mid = y + LINE / 2;
  if (it.kind === 'anniversary') {
    g.fillStyle = '#e377c2';                     // a little flag
    g.fillRect(x + 1, y + 3, 8, 6);
    g.fillRect(x + 1, y + 3, 2, LINE - 5);
    text(g, it.text, x + 14, mid, w - 14, '12px Homerton');
  } else {
    text(g, it.time || '', x, mid, 40, 'bold 12px Homerton');
    text(g, it.text, x + 42, mid, w - 42, '12px Homerton');
  }
  diary.box(x, y, w, LINE, it.ref);
}
</PRE>

